  "text_md": "Remark 3. According to Remark 2, each I ij can be written as an interval I ij = [ L ij , U ij ] where L ij and U ij are the lower and upper bounds of the interval, respectively. Also, each S ij (if it is non-empty) is obtained as one of the forms of S ij = { L ij } , S ij = { U ij } , S ij = { L ij , U ij } or S ij = [ L ij , U ij ].\n\nDefinition 4. For each j ∈ J , we define I j = ⋂ i ∈ I I ij . Also, let S ′ ij = S ij ∩ I j , ∀ i ∈ I and ∀ j ∈ J .\n\nCorollary 4. Let i ∈ I and j ∈ J . If S ′ ij = ∅ , then S ′ ij is as one of the forms of S ′ ij = { L j } , S ′ ij = { U j } , S ′ ij = { L j , U j } or S ′ ij = [ L j , U j ], where L j and U j are the lower and upper bound of I j = [ L j , U j ], respectively.\n\n̸\n\nProof. From Definition 4 and Remark 3, we have\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n̸\n\nHence, for each i ∈ I , if L ij ∈ I j ( U ij ∈ I j ), then we must have L ij = L j ( U ij = U j ). On the other hand, from Remark 3, a non-empty S ij is as one of the forms of S ij = { L ij } , S ij = { U ij } , S ij = { L ij , U ij } or S ij = [ L ij , U ij ]. So, if S ij = { L ij } and S ′ ij = S ij ∩ I j = ∅ , then L ij ∈ I j ; that is, L ij = L j and therefore S ′ ij = S ij ∩ I j = { L j } . For the other cases, the results are attained by the similar argument.\n\nExample 1. Consider Problem (1) includes Yager operator where\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n| 0 . 25   | 0 . 32   | 0 . 41   | 0 . 19   | 0 . 70   | 0 . 13   | 0 . 44   | 0 . 37   | 0 . 28   | 0 . 50   |\n|----------|----------|----------|----------|----------|----------|----------|----------|----------|----------|\n| 0 . 80   | 0 . 73   | 0 . 64   | 0 . 79   | 0 . 80   | 0 . 22   | 0 . 80   | 0 . 56   | 0 . 10   | 0 . 28   |\n| 0 . 11   | 0 . 20   | 0 . 12   | 0 . 13   | 0 . 05   | 0 . 25   | 0 . 40   | 0 . 25   | 0 . 20   | 0 . 18   |\n| 0 . 10   | 0 . 23   | 0 . 25   | 0 . 15   | 0 . 12   | 0 . 05   | 0 . 02   | 0 . 01   | 0 . 15   | 0 . 15   |\n| 0 . 45   | 0 . 35   | 0 . 70   | 